<br/>
If you mainly want a smart watch for notifications, standard workout and sleep tracking, and an always-on display, the &zwnj;Apple Watch SE 3&zwnj; remains the better value. Although it is now a year old, it still represents excellent value for money at &#36;249, offering most of the Apple Watch's core features for &#36;150 less than the entry-level Series 12.<br/>
<br/>
The Apple Watch Series 12 has a much stronger case for anyone who uses their device to follow health and recovery trends. Its new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/apple-watch-series-12-and-ultra-4-health-and-fitness/">Health Sensing System</a> enables frequent heart rate readings, Recovery HRV, and a Readiness score that draws on recent activity, training load, vitals, and sleep. ECG, blood oxygen monitoring, and hypertension notifications add further capabilities unavailable on the &zwnj;Apple Watch SE 3&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
Battery life may be a decisive factor. The Apple Watch Series 12 is rated for six more hours of normal use and three more hours of outdoor workout tracking. It also charges faster, which is particularly useful if you wear your watch overnight for sleep tracking.<br/>
<br/>
The Series 12 also offers a larger and brighter display, which makes a noticeable difference in daily use, along with 5GHz Wi-Fi, Precision Finding for a misplaced iPhone, and premium titanium and ceramic case options. Its <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/audio-intelligence-features/">Audio Intelligence</a> features include &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Recap, Sound Recognition, and always-on Shazam.<br/>
<br/>
